title: Is this part of a Staal-Crowde conspiracy?
subtitle: Rumor has it that the chancellor is planning a *Querfront*, and Strasserist split in the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span> is just the beginning.
on-arrival: royalist_peak_triggered = 1 if not gallax_mode; royalist_peak_votes = royalist_votes  if not gallax_mode; royalist_decay_timer = 24 if not gallax_mode; coup_progress += 4; commons_royalist -= 2; disfavored_royalist -= 2; royalist_explode += 1

However, nothing ever happens. After sending a letter to [+ royalist_leader +] explaining his reasons for resigning, Crowde quietly departs on a two-week vacation to northern the southern realms. In a letter, he assures [+ royalist_leader +] that he has no intention of becoming "the focal point of oppositional efforts or even of discussions thereof." Crowde is not a fighter like his brother; he has squandered his chance to trigger a meaningful defection—potentially involving sixty Assembly deputies.

This was not part of any grand plan by Staal to split the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span>. In fact, the loss of his closest potential "ally" within the party has effectively ended Staal’s hopes of using his influence to convince the broader party to tolerate his government.

For the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span>, losing their most effective organizational leader is a serious blow. Across the Commonwealth, a growing sense emerges: the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Royalists</span> have peaked, and their internal contradictions will soon begin pitting the movement against itself. However, Gallax’s threat of suicide, his impassioned pleas, and his charismatic presence have so far held the party together—preventing others from following Crowde’s lead, at least for now. Publicly, Gallax claims that Crowde has merely requested sick leave, softening the immediate impact.

> *"If the party falls apart... I’ll finish myself off with a pistol within three minutes."*

 - Alaric Gallax to Joseph Standerton, [+ year_display +]

# not immediate support loss, since strasser and nsdap didnt make a big deal over it to the public